 Every Remote endpoint is access-controlled: a Peer whose principal is not the owner reaches an endpoint only when, on every resource the call touches, a binding of that principal kind's policy in [`dsh-api-remote-access`](../remote-access/README.md) grants a role listing the action ([Peer principals Agent Note](../../../.agents/notes/implemented/architecture/2026-10-12-peer-principal-and-remote-access.md)). The controller injects `remoteAccess` and registers the `session` resource kind with the default field `sessionId` through `typert.resources`. `session/page` and `session/follow` declare a `session` reader in `@Remote({ resources })` that reads `request.address.sessionId` for an ordinary Session address and `request.address.childSessionId` for a subagent address; whether access to the parent Session covers its children is the policy's rule. `session/projections` reads the Session resource from `request.sessionId`, so a reconnecting reader reloads only the projections of Sessions it may read. `session/list`, `session/search`, and `session/control` read no resource, so their calls touch the `global` resource and need a `global` role; for a non-owner caller, `session/list` returns only the rows, `session/search` only the hits, and `session/control` only the baseline projections and projection frames, whose Session `ctx.remoteAccess.allows()` admits for the same action, so that action also belongs to a `session` role; owner results are unfiltered. `session/prompt` builds the durable `user-rpc` message source in its Remote method and records `principal` only for a non-owner caller, read through `principalOf(this.ctx.invocation)`; a source without `principal` belongs to the owner.
 
